From dhunter at stat.psu.edu  Fri Oct 19 22:39:34 2007
From: dhunter at stat.psu.edu (David Hunter)
Date: Fri Oct 19 22:39:34 2007
Subject: [statnet_help] Controlling the reference group in "nodefactor"
In-Reply-To: <Pine.WNT.4.64.0710171051350.2680@vinaya.csde.washington.edu>
References: <20071016105210.yi5hauru9rwc4ss8@webmail.mit.edu>
	<Pine.WNT.4.64.0710171051350.2680@vinaya.csde.washington.edu>
Message-ID: <22F17FF2-41FC-4F4B-A92C-28B19BD9855A@stat.psu.edu>

Hi Brian.

I coded this.  It will be in the new version of statnet when it is  
released, but in the meantime, you should be able to get it to work  
if you simply copy and paste the attached version of the  
InitErgm.nodefactor function into R.  You can also use the "source"  
function for this, if you know about that.  This will replace the old  
version of InitErgm.nodefactor.  (You may have to do this every time  
you start a new session, depending on whether you save your  
workspace.  But beware:  If you keep this stopgap around and it is  
always in your workspace, then it will always override the "official"  
version, even if we make future mods to the "official" version.)

There is a new (optional) argument to nodefactor called "omitwhich".   
By default, its value is 1.  However, you can replace it by any  
integer (or vector of integers) you want.  What it does is to tell  
nodefactor which levels of the factor (numbered in order, sorted from  
lowest to highest) to eliminate.  If you have a 4-level factor with  
levels "Orange", "Apple", "Banana", "Pear", then by default "Apple"  
will be omitted; but if you want to put only indicators for apples  
and pears in your model, you can omit bananas and oranges (which are  
numbers 2 and 3 in the sorted list) by typing

  ... + nodefactor ("fruit", omitwhich = c(2,3)) + ...

Careful, there is no check to ensure that you don't do something  
crazy with omitwhich.

Hope this helps!
Dave


