Subject: [statnet_help] How to asses convergence a possible bug
Message-ID: <4721B9CF.80008@gmail.com>

Dear list.
Can anyone give me quick pointers on how to asses it the convergence of 
the ergm model is good. I have used the function mcmc.diagnostics, but 
am not sure how to read the output. I assume that if the convergence is 
good, then:
1. The output should say that there is "enough" both burn-in and samples.
2. The plots should stabilize after some point (btw, I have a problem 
when there is more than e.g. 3 terms in the model, not all plot are 
shown (I believe all are plotted, but the first ones are rewritten by 
the last ones) and some plots are very small).
3. The Dependence factor (I) should be low (lower than 5???). I 
frequently get dependenc factors in thousands. Any suggestions?

Are there any other options. Are there any suggestions what can be done 
besides selecting better starting values, increasing MCMC sample size 
and selecting terms that are not correlated?

Somethimes, when I run "mcmc.diagnostics", I get "NaN" for some terms in 
the correlations and the convergence statistics is missing for these 
terms. These are the same terms that have NA for MCMC s.e. in summary 
output.  What does this mean.

Aslo, when I sepcify theta0 (starting values that are therefore not 
estimated using maximum pseudo-likelihood ), I get the following error 
when I run the summary on ergm object:
"Error in -object$mplefit$hessian : invalid argument to unary operator"
Probably because the ergm object does not have the element "mplefit".


Best regarads,
Ales Ziberna


From br263 at cornell.edu  Mon Nov  5 10:09:37 2007
From: br263 at cornell.edu (Brian Rubineau)
Date: Mon Nov  5 10:09:37 2007
Subject: [statnet_help] comparing parameter estimates across models
Message-ID: <3498.128.84.27.104.1194286177.squirrel@webmail.cornell.edu>

Greetings.
I have data with one set of nodes and two sets of edges
(friendship and academic ties among the same set of subjs).

I am using two instances of the same ergm (except for the DV)
to predict the two edge-sets.

I'd like to statistically compare the parameter estimates
across the two models (e.g., is racial homophily significantly
stronger for friendship vs. academic ties?).

The Chow test adapted to logistic regression requires pooling
data, and I've been thinking about trying this using the union
of the two edgesets and edge-covariate dummies to indicate the
source edgeset, or pooling and adding an interaction term, but
this is somewhat beyond my depth.

Any suggestions?

Thanks
Brian

